 :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286402}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286318} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea164f5-ab88-5833-8f94-dcbc1b0c609b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53ac5dfb-f75e-5ef2-a84c-e58da3b6698e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181999" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7f7b1dc9bf4da61b573345aaf92fe0d29ea00ef5558a9fca985d8af25fa0a03.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,2\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>2.\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>2\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "65947926-bb86-51fe-88e7-a8252d5c73a2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ad1af7-5e4b-5b23-8b4a-240b1732cda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433480} {:answer/id #uuid "5092a904-7fb7-5b45-afc3-5d2f76eb301d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433482} {:answer/id #uuid "546003b5-05e8-5b2c-b100-ec0f104c1909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433483} {:answer/id #uuid "a147a2b2-ac26-5864-a695-452d2ee464cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433487} {:answer/id #uuid "d48d53eb-2eb5-59fd-bc2b-f89816dbd891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433487} :answer-field/id #uuid "6778b0be-3214-5289-bc42-c4d9931ed8b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433489} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41080398-b2ba-56b8-9658-937f33ed7f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433481} {:answer/id #uuid "64c0d4ca-3551-5246-9474-bf87dc44f240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433484} {:answer/id #uuid "76d2c0ab-aee8-5245-b31a-1656f6a34d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433485} {:answer/id #uuid "a114218b-7eb8-53d8-acac-162934598611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433485} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebda1f69-fee2-52dd-85c4-c20bfd2e3b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ee4392fe-5c34-5631-8fcd-93d0be346505" :question/math-academy-id "q-181693" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f9807dae43519dbd9407130c51cb1473a36c46a42d8c433d9b4d88edb4e382d.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,2{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>2{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>2{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c0749e21-8c06-5ff7-9468-c3ffd9b7e5a8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9099832-9536-5764-b8f9-82d9f50c96e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409896} :answer-field/id #uuid "1979d639-7c7b-51e8-9e83-40721047de2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409897} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53607ff6-5913-50ea-923c-e269930ccd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409894} :answer-field/id #uuid "38cdacac-1881-5ec8-b9e9-d206d7013904" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d74e258e-f0dd-579c-9148-3c83bfa32b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409895} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1c0edf-ff99-50f4-9ecf-942f2ee605d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6164b43d-cb18-5fe7-a896-e7c6ff8846af" :question/math-academy-id "q-181810" :question/problem "![](images/59/592dc9d0d4713406cf9bf9c6ad966cb4446420113aa0e94fcfdcdb35b6471a9c.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dcfbe27c-c8e2-5c19-b864-9a5ca4314ece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b61e05-59a4-5b3f-b404-1a91178b7595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284796} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9cd160-1c52-5abb-b208-1d5e10d1cad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285219} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6176b9-6955-525f-a996-4e3f9ea2afb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604549